\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Lifted By Yen, Bonds Extend Losses  \b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2006\par 09:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar extended gains Friday, reacting to a strong Asian rally in the Japanese yen on expectations the Bank of Japan may soon end its ultra-easy monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds declined, reacting to overnight losses in U.S. Treasurys and concerns over Singapore's inflation, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0647 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6250, slightly down from S$1.6253 in late New York trade Thursday. It traded at S$1.6288 in late Asian trade Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar moved between S$1.6226 and S$1.6272 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was tied to the movements in the dollar-yen,  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen rallied after BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui told a government financial committee Thursday conditions for ending the quantitative easing policy are "gradually ripening."  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the U.S. unit briefly dropped to Y116.42 - the lowest level since Y116.12 on Jan. 27 - after selling by U.S. banks and macrofunds triggered stop-loss orders around Y116.50, traders said. It had closed Thursday's New York session at Y117.11.  \par \par \uc2 However a dealer at Singapore bank said the U.S. dollar erased some of its early losses after having slipped to S$1.6226 on fears \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 January's inflation data for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  that showed consumer price index rising 1.7% from a year earlier in January - the largest gain since November 2004 - has also made players bullish about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite belief that the local unit is on the stronger side of trade-weighted band, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore had forecast inflation to remain between 0.5% and 1.5% in 2006, up from 0.5% last year.  \par \par \uc2 Higher prices and a strong economy has increased the likelihood of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifting to a tighter monetary policy and allowing a steeper appreciation of the Singapore dollar's trade-weighted exchange rate, bankers said.  \par \par \uc2 DBS Bank said any upside surprise on inflation in the run-up to the April policy announcement could increase the risk of a tighter monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 "This could be carried out via a steeper appreciation bias or a level shift of the (Singapore dollar) exchange rate. Since the start of the year, the SGD nominal effective exchange rate has been trading on the strong side of the band," DBS said.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on the two-year bond rose eight basis points to 2.82% - its fourth consecutive rise - while the 10-year bond yield rose four basis points to 3.36% after losses in U.S. Treasurys overnight and concerns over inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's auction for sale of S$1.1 billion 10-year government bonds also increased anxiety as the cutoff yield of 3.45% showed market participants weren't keen to buy bonds at lower yields.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        3.125%    3.250% \par Two-year bond yield   2.82%     2.74% \par 10-year bond yield    3.36%     3.32% \par Two-year swap offer   3.48%     3.46% \par 10-year swap offer    3.62%     3.59% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  14 BPs    13 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historica