\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Up Late On Positive Data Expectations  \b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Februar 2006\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose against its U.S. counterpart in Asia Tuesday, bolstered by expectations of strong major domestic data later this week.  \par \par \uc2 The government bond yield curve flattened, in line with Monday's inversion of the U.S. Treasury curve.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6269, down from Monday's New York close of S$1.6294. It was quoted at S$1.6303 in Asia Monday.  \par \par \uc2 From early in the session, the U.S. dollar gradually fell against the Singapore dollar, eventually hitting an intraday low of S$1.6265 late afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 The market expects Wednesday's retail sales and Thursday's final reading for fourth-quarter gross domestic product to indicate robust economic growth, said dealers.  \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank expects December retail sales growth to pick up to 9.0% on year, from 2.4% in November. 'This will be boosted in part by the rebound in vehicle sales, given the increase in new car registrations in December,' it said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's non-oil domestic exports for January, though, is likely to indicate some moderation from December's torrid 31.6% on-year growth, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 A further fall of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar may be limited by possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Although there were no apparent signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by late local trade Tuesday, one trader cited rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought U.S. dollars via local banks in Asia late Monday to curb a sharp rise in the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Yields on short-dated Singapore government bonds rose while those on very long-term issues fell, leading to mild curve flattening, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Proprietary traders were rumored to have bought 15-year government bonds, dragging their yield down to 3.38% Tuesday from 3.40% Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        1.3750%   2.3750% \par Two-year bond yield   2.66%     2.63% \par 10-year bond yield    3.27%     3.27% \par Two-year swap offer   3.34%     3.37% \par 10-year swap offer    3.56%     3.60% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  22 BPs    23 BPs \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4159; ron.harui@dowjones.com -Edited by Clarissa Tan -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060214e22e00691\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
