\par\par \b \uc2 Weak dollar forces Asia's hand on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2006\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Feb 8 (Reuters) - Just when a large stockpile of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and strong domestic demand was finally weaning Asia off its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  habit, weakness in the U.S. dollar has thrown a spanner in the works.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed Asia's reserves, excluding China's, jumped in January by $29 billion, more than in any month in the past year, as a weaker U.S. dollar and portfolio inflows forced central banks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.  \par \par \uc2  South Korea's $6.5 billion growth in reserves in January was the highest since December 2004, Singapore's $3.55 billion increase was the biggest since April last year and the Philippines reported its largest monthly gain in reserves on record, taking total Asian reserves to just over $2.7 trillion.  \par \par \uc2  The figures were evidence of the overwhelming flows the region has received, giving central banks little choice but to absorb some of them and temper the rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2  Even with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Korean won <KRW=> rose nearly 5 percent in January, the Thai baht <THB=> rose 5 percent and the Singapore dollar <SGD=> 2 percent.  \par \par \uc2  "January was clearly a month of enormous pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate. I would have been surprised if Asian central banks hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Merrill Lynch's head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, Simon Flint.  \par \par \uc2  It could be a sign of how 2006 will shape up on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy front.  \par \par \uc2  Asia's reserves grew by just $251 billion in 2005, compared with $538 billion in 2004 and $473 billion in 2003, as Japan stopped buying dollars and other regional central banks grew less obsessed with export-driven growth and more tolerant of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.  \par \par \uc2  Besides, the dollar was rising through most of 2005.  \par \par \uc2  But, with U.S. rates probably close to a peak, the dollar started 2006 on a weak footing. If that trend persists and strong growth in the United States and elsewhere leads to higher Asian trade surpluses and portfolio investment in Asian markets, the region's reserves could very well surge, as they did in 2003 and 2004, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2  "A number of countries, Korea in particular, are trying to move away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," Flint said.  \par \par \uc2  "They intend to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less. It's just that the market conditions dictate that they continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly."  \par \par \uc2  STRONGER \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Asia has a number of reasons to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less and let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise faster, the main one being the still high cost of the oil the region imports.  \par \par \uc2  Also, analysts believe China and Malaysia are allowing a faster pace of appreciation in the yuan and ringgit than immediately after they unlinked the two from the dollar in July 2005.  \par \par \uc2  Growth is more broad-based, with consumption and investment demand picking up and reducing Asia's reliance on exports. Japan's deflationary pressures have abated.  \par \par \uc2  Moreover, rates in the United States may peak just as Asia steps up monetary tightening. That means the cost of issuing paper to absorb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  released in dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known as sterilisation, could mount just as returns on dollar assets stop rising.  \par \par \uc2  "The current environment of increased inflation risk should prompt Asian countries to be more willing to accept domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. So should ongoing, albeit modest, yuan appreciation," said Jan Lambregts, head of Asia-Pacific research at Rabobank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  therefore should not be as big a factor in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as in 2003 and 2004, Lambregts said in a note.  \par \par \uc2  "At the same time, there's some early evidence of Asian countries falling back on bad \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  habits in the face of the U.S. dollar's weak start to the year that warrants monitoring," he wrote.  (For a table detailing Asian countries' reserves, click on [nSP89186])    \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ASIA-RESERVES|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|Z|J|SNI|SNS|SI|PHD|IF|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060208e228000ow\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
