\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Weakness on US rate view seen brief  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2006\par 03:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Feb 2 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were broadly weaker on Thursday, retreating from the previous day's multi-year peaks as markets pushed the dollar higher to price in more U.S. rate rises.  \par \par \uc2 But most analysts said weakness in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be short-lived because flows into regional equity markets were strong and positioning in favour of the regionals was still light.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose to a seven-week high above 118 yen <JPY=> and also rallied against the euro <EUR=> and other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as interest rate markets priced in further monetary tightening in the United States this year.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve raised the fed funds rate on Tuesday for the 14th time in 19 months and hinted further rises could follow.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> dropped a percent on the day to around 974 a dollar, pulling further back from Wednesday's eight-year peak around 957.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> and the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> were also big losers. The Singapore dollar slipped to the weaker side of 1.63 per U.S. dollar, ceding more than a cent from the previous day's high.  \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> rose early to 52.03 per dollar, its strongest level since May 2003, and then slipped. It has risen about 1.8 percent since Jan. 23, buoyed by expectations of foreign portfolio inflows and a possible upgrade in the country's credit ratings.  \par \par \uc2 Markets in China and Taiwan were closed for Lunar New Year holidays.  \par \par \uc2 Sean Callow, Westpac Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said the dollar's rebound was no surprise.  \par \par \uc2 "The market did get a little bit carried away with the notion that the dollar should decline as soon as the Fed even hints at finishing tightening.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no guarantee that they have finished hiking yet and that the yield story will be broadly supportive of the dollar overall," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the strength of economic fundamentals would support \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the won, ensuring their losses were limited, Callow said.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar/won could track higher in the short-term but it is hard to argue for a major pullback on any of the won crosses. The fundamental story for the won is very strong," he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Analysts suspected central banks in Korea, India, the Philippines and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to rein in the strength of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "They are never going to sit passively while markets take their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger against the dollar," Tim Condon, ING's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist said.  \par \par \uc2 "They are always going to be dollar defenders but I suspect that means they slow but not reverse the pressure," he said, while citing the strength of flows underpinning Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves jumped $6.5 billion in January, the biggest monthly gain in 14 months, which dealers said indicated heavy dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of Korea.  \par \par \uc2 Yet, the commerce ministry said on Wednesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been complacent and had allowed the won to strengthen rapidly.  \par \par \uc2 This week, the won hit an 8-year high near 8.14 per yen <JPYKRW=R>, as Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outperformed the yen.  \par \par \uc2 ABN AMRO Bank's Shahab Jalinoos said the outperformance against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would persist.  \par \par \uc2 "This reflects the fact that low-yielding yen is currently a global funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a risk-loving environment, while Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are beneficiaries of risk-seeking global capital seeking returns in their asset markets," he said in a note. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0603 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.49         117.67       -0.69\par  Sing dlr        1.6333         1.6263       -0.43\par  Korean won      972.00         962.00       -1.03\par  Baht             39.39          39.20       -0.48\par  Peso             52.08          52.09       +0.02\par  Rupiah         9345.00        9310.00       -0.37\par  Rupee            44.24          44.20       -0.08\par  Ringgit         3.7480         3.7495       +0.04\par  Change so far in 2006\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.49         117.96       -0.45\par  Sing dlr        1.6333         1.6625       +1.79\par  Korean won      972.00        1011.60       +4.07\par  Baht             39.39          41.11       +4.37\par  Peso             52.08          53.09       +1.94\par  Rupiah         9345.00        9835.00       +5.24\par  Rupee            44.24          45.04       +1.82\par  Ringgit         3.7480    