\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Up Late; Rumored  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Curbs Rise  \b0\par\par\uc2 516 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2006\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar rose in Asia Wednesday on foreign fund flows into Singapore equities and domestic corporate demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  curbed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds were mostly weaker, tracking Tuesday's fall in U.S. Treasury prices, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0706 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6221, down from Tuesday's New York close of S$1.6227. In Asian trade Tuesday, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.6216.  \par \par \uc2 From early in Wednesday's Asian session, the U.S. dollar fell steadily against the Singapore dollar, eventually hitting a new 10-month low of S$1.6187 in the afternoon. The S$1.6187 level was the lowest since March 14, 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts said foreign investors' flows into the Singapore stock market and local companies' demand for Singapore dollars ahead of the coming Lunar New Year holiday were behind the U.S. dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners typically invest in Asian stock markets such as Singapore's Straits Times Index to build long Asian equity positions to hold over the holidays, one local analyst said. At 0706 GMT, the Straits Times Index was up 16.75 points at 2385.07.  \par \par \uc2 In the run-up to the Lunar New Year holiday which starts Jan. 28 and ends Jan. 31 in Singapore, local corporations have also been buying Singapore dollars, as many of them traditionally give money out as gifts in red envelopes at this time of year.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar then recovered to around the S$1.6220 level late in the session on suspected U.S. dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the S$1.6190-S$1.6195 zone to temper the Singapore unit's sharp rise, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Singapore government bonds were mostly pulled up by Tuesday's mild rise in U.S. Treasury yields, but local trading was thin due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's auction of S$2.4 billion worth of five-year government bonds, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Around mid-day, the MAS sold the five-year bonds at a cutoff yield of 3.15%, in line with market expectations.  \par \par \uc2 Bids totaled S$4.78 billion and the average yield was 3.09%, according to data posted on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Web site.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        2.9375%   3.0625% \par Two-year bond yield   2.74%     2.73% \par 10-year bond yield    3.33%     3.31% \par Two-year swap offer   3.28%     3.28% \par 10-year swap offer    3.61%     3.61% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  33 BPS    33 BPs \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4159; ron.harui@dowjones.com -Edited by Michael Kitchen -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060125e21p00691\par }\p