\par\par \b \uc2 Sing dlr hovers near 10-mth high, barrier at 1.62/dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2006\par 03:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jan 24 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar traded on Tuesday near a 10-month high reached in late Asian trading the previous day, as investors moved out of U.S. dollar assets on signs that U.S. interest rates were close to peaking.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> traded as strong as 1.6205 per dollar, close to Monday's 10-month high of 1.6195, as the U.S. dollar dropped to a four-month low against the euro <EUR=>.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said local banks were buying U.S. dollars in late trading on Monday, apparently on direction from the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at S$1.62.  \par \par \uc2 "The local banks were \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite aggressively yesterday evening to support the 1.62 level," said a Singapore dealer.  \par \par \uc2 "This morning those who were short dollars were buying back on seeing their inability to break the 1.62 level."  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said there was little evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trading on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 They said the Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were unlikely to gain on their own merit because foreign inflows into their stock markets have ebbed after a spurt at the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 But the Singapore dollar could break the 1.62 level if the U.S. dollar falls below 114 yen, a dealer said. The dollar traded as low as 114.3 yen on Tuesday, compared with 114.62 yen in late Asian trading on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped to a four-month low against the euro and Swiss franc <CHF=> after Federal Reserve officials, including St. Louis Fed President William Poole and New York Fed President Timothy Geithner, suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 18-month tightening cycle is nearly over and pointed to the dangers of U.S. deficits.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -SINGAPORE|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060124e21o0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
