\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Weakens Late On US Dlr Short-Covering  \b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2006\par 08:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was moderately weaker in Asia Friday, as interbank players bought back the U.S. dollar in the wake of Thursday's better-than-expected U.S. data.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds weakened across the board, bucking Thursday's gains in U.S. Treasury prices, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0620 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6312, up from S$1.6223 in Asia Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 After opening near its intraday low of S$1.6271, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose as high as S$1.6328 in the morning as interbank players covered short U.S. dollar positions against the local dollar, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Sparking this U.S. dollar short-covering was U.S. Commerce Department data released Thursday that showed the November trade deficit contracted to US$64.21 billion from a revised record high of US$68.13 billion in October. Economists had forecast a US$66.5 billion deficit for November.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also said some market participants sold Singapore dollars on the view that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had climbed to the upper limit of the band within which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The MAS operates a managed float regime for Singapore, which allows the trade-weighted exchange rate to fluctuate within an undisclosed policy band and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent it from breaching the band.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds, which closely track U.S. Treasurys, defied expectations for price gains Friday, as their prices slipped across the curve on selling by local banks, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 One trader said a large-sized domestic bank was behind the local bond sell-off, possibly aimed at taking profit on recent gains in local bond prices.  \par \par \uc2 'We are not following the U.S. today, and most of the trades are betting on a steepening of the curve,' the dealer added.  \par \par \uc2 Curve steepening trades have picked up following recent robust economic data such as the city-state's gross domestic product, which expanded at a faster-than-expected pace of 7.7% in the fourth quarter of 2005 from the same period of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The 7.7% growth figure was better than the average forecast of 6.7% by six economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        3.3125%   3.3755% \par Two-year bond yield   2.81%     2.79% \par 10-year bond yield    3.32%     3.30% \par Two-year swap offer   3.34%     3.37% \par 10-year swap offer    3.64%     3.65% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  30 BPS    28 BPs \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4159; ron.harui@dowjones.com (Saeed Azhar in Singapore contributed to this story.) -Edited by -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020060113e21d004s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
