\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Hits Strongest Since Mar  \b0\par\par\uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2006\par 08:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2006, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar surged to an almost 10-month high Monday, tracking sharp gains in the Japanese yen against the U.S. dollar on signs U.S. interest rate increases may end soon.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds edged up slightly in thin trade as a further easing of the overnight rate and a local holiday Tuesday kept investors on the sidelines, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0609 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6324, down from S$1.6464 in Asia Friday.  \par \par \uc2 This was the lowest level for the U.S. dollar since March 22, when it dipped as low as S$1.6291.  \par \par \uc2 Intraday the U.S. dollar traded between S$1.6322 and S$1.6389.  \par \par \uc2 A dealer at a European bank said the local pair rallied as it tracked gains in the yen against the U.S. dollar triggered by Friday's weaker-than-expected December U.S. non-farm payrolls number.  \par \par \uc2 In late Asian trade, the U.S. dollar was at Y113.84, down from Y114.39 in New York Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Philip Wee, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at DBS Bank, said the rally in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was triggered by an unwinding of carry trades, especially in the Japanese yen.  \par \par \uc2 Last year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market undertook massive carry trades - where market players borrow in a cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Japanese yen and buy high-interest yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The trades began after China disappointed the market by appreciating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a lower-than-expected 2% and on expectations the Federal Reserve would aggressively tighten monetary policy which would support the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Recent signals from the Fed suggest the rate hikes may be coming to an end, leading to the unwinding of these carry trades, Wee said.  \par \par \uc2 But traders are also worried the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's trade-weighted exchange rate from breaching the band's upper limit.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar was 1.3% above the midpoint of the band - the highest level since April 2004 when the MAS first tightened policy - according to a report from United Overseas Bank.  \par \par \uc2 UOB treasury economist Jimmy Koh expects the U.S. dollar to find strong support at S$1.6300 while it should stabilize against the yen around Y113.50.  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, the 10-year yield fell one basis point to 3.24%. The two-year yield also slid one basis point to 2.74%.  \par \par \uc2 While the yield curve was unchanged, JPMorgan said the recent easing of short-term rates should encourage curve steepening trades. The overnight rate dropped to 2.250% from 2.625% Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        2.250%    2.625% \par Two-year bond yield   2.74%     2.75% \par 10-year bond yield    3.24%     3.25% \par Two-year swap offer   3.25%     3.31% \par 10-year swap offer    2.51%     3.58% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  26 BPS    27 BPs \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*