\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  may have less bite this time  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Rajat Bhattacharya  \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2006\par 09:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 6 (Reuters) - Asian central banks have made it clear they are not happy with a surge in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as 2006 gets under way, but analysts doubt they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a large scale, saying economic strength warrants firmer exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea vowed on Friday to do its utmost to stall a jump of nearly 3 percent in the won against the dollar in the new year while the central banks of Taiwan and Singapore sold dollars to halt a month-old rally in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaked in 2004 -- Japan leading the region with massive dollar purchases in an attempt to hold the yen down -- as Japan and South Korea struggled to pull out of a prolonged economic downturn.  \par \par \uc2 Asian authorities were worried that strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would make their exports less competitive, which would hamper the recovery of their export-dependent economies.  \par \par \uc2 Asia's top 10 holders of foreign reserves together bought $44 billion a month on average that year.  \par \par \uc2 This time it's different, analysts said. The rise in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is part of a broad dollar decline, triggered by expectations that U.S. interest rates may be close to peaking, and Asian economies are on a stronger footing.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is not afraid of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Roh Sang Chil, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Kookmin Bank in Seoul.  \par \par \uc2 "The fundamentals of the Korean economy are getting better. There's not much reason for the Korean won to depreciate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to be just a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 The same holds true for the rest of Asia, analysts said, where economies from Taiwan to India are set to accelerate thanks to an upturn in domestic demand while China remains the engine of growth in the region. The improving outlook, which contrasts with an expected slowdown in the United States, has led to a rush of foreign equity investors into India, Taiwan, Thailand and other Asian countries in the past month.  \par \par \uc2 Stock indices in South Korea <.KS11>, India <.BSESN> and Indonesia <.JKSE> hit record highs this week while those in Singapore <.STI>, Thailand <.SETI> and the Philippines <.PSI> reached or approached multi-year peaks.  \par \par \uc2 "Hence the underlying flows remain solidly in favour of regional Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Shahab Jalinoos, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO Bank, said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been rising against the U.S. dollar since December. The foreign equity inflows in the new year merely accelerated the process, with the won <KRW=> hitting an eight-year high of 985.1 per dollar on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 BROAD-BASED MOVE  \par \par \uc2 Claudio Piron, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorganChase Bank, noted that this year's Asian rally differed from rallies in 2004 and the early part of 2005, which were driven by speculation about a sharp appreciation in the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 This time the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are moving more in line with the yen, an indication that their gains are more broad-based and can be sustained, Piron said.  \par \par \uc2 "We seem to be having a broader move, which the yen is joining in, and it is always hard to turn that tide," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems to be driven by equity flows and that's premised on an upward assessment of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Some believe the rise in the won and the Taiwan dollar may have run its course for the time being. Roh at Kookmin Bank said the Bank of Korea was likely to defend the 990 won per dollar level for now.  \par \par \uc2 But, analysts said, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have more room to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 "For those wishing to be short dollar/Asia, it seems safer at this stage to look at the Indian rupee and especially the baht and rupiah rather than the won and Taiwan dollar, which are already overstretched," said Jalinoos at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 Others think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bark may be stronger than its bite this time round.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past few years has already bloated foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in many countries and adding to them would be both expensive and counterproductive.  \par \par \uc2 "There is increased concern about the rising cost to the Bank of Korea from holding large amounts of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," Merrill Lynch said, adding that the won's appreciation had not had a significantly detrimental impact on the South Korean economy. Asian central banks, including the Bank of Korea, have been raising interest rates to quell inflation pressures and Japan is set to end its ultra-loose monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Efforts to reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation -- which typically loosens monetary conditions -- would run counter to those policies.  \par \par \uc2 "The amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far has not been that significant and the danger is that if Korea and Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily, they could create an opportunity for the market," said Craig Chan, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland. (Additional reporting by Dhara Ranasinghe and Vidya Ranganathan) ((Editing by Alan Raybould; rajat.bhattacharya@reuters.com; Reuters Messaging: rajat.bhattacharya.reuters.com@reuters.net; +65 6870 3841))  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|IF|PHD|SNI|SNS|J|Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060109e216000gd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
