\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Flat Late Ahead Of Year-End Holidays  \b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Dezember 2005\par 07:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was flat against its U.S. counterpart in Asia late Wednesday amid extremely thin trading ahead of year-end holidays.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices rose across the curve, in line with U.S. Treasurys' price gains Tuesday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0615 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6665, unchanged from Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6662 and S$1.6688 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Throughout the Asian session, the U.S. dollar's movements versus the Singapore dollar were somewhat choppy within a narrow range of roughly 26 pips, tracking the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fluctuations against the yen, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Traders added that the absence of many Singaporean and foreign players due to year-end holidays was the main factor behind the very low volume of trading.  \par \par \uc2 "Nobody is trading" the U.S. dollar against the Singapore dollar, a dealer with a Singapore-based foreign bank said.  \par \par \uc2 But, recent robust Singapore data such as the better-than-expected 22.4% on-year growth in industrial output for November reported Tuesday have spurred expectations among some analysts for a stronger Singapore dollar in the short term.  \par \par \uc2 The trade-weighted Singapore dollar "still has an upward bias toward the strong side" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's policy band, DBS Bank's senior market strategist, Philip Wee, said. He estimated the trade-weighted Singapore dollar is about 0.7% above the perceived midpoint of the band.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS allows the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar to fluctuate within an undisclosed policy band, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from breaching the band.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts monetary policy through the exchange rate rather than interest rates because trade amounts to three times the country's S$184 billion domestic economy.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest    Previous \par Overnight rate        3.125%    2.875% \par Two-year bond yield   2.70%     2.71% \par 10-year bond yield    3.25%     3.29% \par Two-year swap offer   3.20%     3.23% \par 10-year swap offer    3.48%     3.53% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  28 BPS    30 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4159; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Genevieve Soledad [ 28-12-05 0630GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1062 20711 4171  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051228e1cs0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
