\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Singapore dlr at 4-mth high, Indian rupee up  \b0\par\par\uc2 915 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2005\par 05:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Dec 19 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar rose to a four-month high on Monday thanks to a year-end sell-off in the U.S. dollar, while the Indian rupee hit a 1-1/2-month high as foreign investors spurred local stocks to record highs.  \par \par \uc2 Similar stock buying pushed the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> to a three-month peak of 33.104 per dollar. But concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in to check the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise checked the gains by midday.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by central banks or indications that authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented stronger appreciation in the Indian rupee <INR=IN>, South Korean won <KRW=>, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar <SGD=> and Thai baht <THB=>.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures show the smoothening out of the exaggerated moves in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past few weeks rather than any efforts to cap those moves," said Jimmy Koh, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at United Overseas Bank in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the Asian central banks want to be that aggressive."  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have rallied this month on the back of the yen's <JPY=> recovery against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 As the year drew to a close, investors who had borrowed low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the yen and Singapore dollar earlier in the year to buy higher-yielding assets such as the U.S. dollar, reversed the carry trades before closing their books.  \par \par \uc2 The reversals have helped the yen climb 4.5 percent since it hit a 2-1/2-month low against the U.S. dollar earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar rose to 1.6635 per dollar on Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strengthened 2.2 percent in the past month, helped by the reversal of the carry trades and strong economic data.  \par \par \uc2 Koh said the trade-weighted Singapore dollar was almost 0.3 percent stronger than the mid-point of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's band for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 CARRY TRADE  \par \par \uc2 "The gains are mainly due to year-end unwinding of carry trades," said a Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer.  \par \par \uc2 The dealer said investors could start borrowing low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the new year to exchange them for the U.S. dollar if markets price in benchmark U.S. interest rates higher than 4.5 percent by the middle of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Another dealer said the carry trades seemed to have run their course and volumes could thin out as year-end holidays approach.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen most of the adjustment for this year," said Koh at United Overseas Bank, adding the yen is likely to trade in the range of 115-120 per dollar going into the new year.  \par \par \uc2 The yen weakened to 116.58 on Monday, but was still close to a seven-week hit earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 In the new year, Koh said, conducting the carry trade by borrowing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to exchange them with U.S. dollars could become riskier as regional interest rates are catching up with those in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 "That'll make shorting of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that much more expensive," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, recent economic data from across Asia indicated the region is heading for another strong quarter following buoyant exports and retail sales in the third quarter. The improved outlook triggered buying of local stocks by foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore's non-oil exports in November rose a higher-than-expected 14 percent to S$12.9 billion from a year earlier, the biggest annual rise in a year, data showed Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Optimism about a strong earnings outlook for Taiwan exporters in the first quarter of 2006 drove the local stock index <.TWII> to a four-month high on Monday. Data showed foreigners have been net buyers of local stocks on all but one day this month.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee got a boost as foreign investors extended their year-long purchases of local stocks, lifting the key stock index <.BSESN> to a new record. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0801 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       116.42         116.25       -0.15\par  Sing dlr        1.6655         1.6695       +0.24\par  Taiwan dlr      33.184         33.174       -0.03\par  Korean won     1017.60        1016.20       -0.14\par  Baht             40.94          40.94       +0.00\par  Peso             53.43          53.45       +0.05\par  Rupiah         9890.00        9885.00       -0.05\par  Rupee            45.17          45.34       +0.37\par  Ringgit         3.7792         3.7793       +0.00\par  Yuan            8.0730         8.0736       +0.01 Change on\par year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       116.42         102.45      -12.00\par  Sing dlr        1.6655         1.6310       -2.07\par  Taiwan dlr      33.184 