\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Rises To 4-Mo High; Bonds Little Changed  \b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2005\par 07:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose to a four-month high against the U.S. dollar Monday as a recent rebound in the Japanese yen and strong economic data for the city-state encouraged players to take fresh positions in the local unit.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond yields were little changed amid thin volume ahead of holidays, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0618 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6658 - its lowest level since Aug. 18 when the greenback slipped to S$1.6631 a few weeks after China switched to a managed float against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , causing the yuan to appreciate 2% against the U.S. dollar. The U.S. dollar traded between S$1.6633 and S$1.6663, down from S$1.6685 late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the Singapore trading session, the U.S. dollar slipped to an intraday low of S$1.6640 after it breached key support of S$1.6645, which triggered stop-losses, said a trader at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 However, a rebound in the U.S. dollar against the yen allowed it to claw back above the Singapore dollar support, said a second dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar had recovered to Y116.35 in afternoon trade after falling as low as Y115.52 in early trade, the weakest since late October.  \par \par \uc2 The trader said fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  also prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar from appreciating more, especially after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore was suspected to have bought back small amounts of U.S. dollars to provide a floor for the U.S. unit Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has gained 2.3% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Nov. 14, when it slipped to S$1.7065, its lowest since early July. It started rallying after a spate of data showed the local economy was strengthening while global markets speculated the Federal Reserve may be nearing the end of its monetary tightening cycle.  \par \par \uc2 "The change in expectations was because of the FOMC statement which encouraged market players to pare back some extended positions in the U.S. dollar going into the new year," said Thomas Lam, a treasury economist at United Overseas Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Lam said the major position-squaring came in the dollar/yen, which also lifted other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Singapore data released Friday showed non-oil domestic exports in November grew 14.0% from a year earlier to S$12.92 billion, beating a Dow Jones Newswires poll forecast of 11.4% growth.  \par \par \uc2 The data also boosted hopes that the trade-dependent economy will be able to sustain strong growth for a second quarter in a row after gross domestic product grew 7% in the third quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank said in a report that the stronger economy means the MAS will likely continue its current policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, which would trigger more gains for the Singapore dollar in the first half of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 On the Singapore government bond market, the 10-year bond yield was flat at 3.34%, while the two-year yield edged up one basis point to 2.77%, flattening the yield curve slightly in thin trade.  \par \par \uc2 The short end has been pressured by liquidity outflows at the end of the year, which has kept the overnight rate firm, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  