s to provide a floor for the U.S. unit Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has gained 2.3% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Nov. 14, when it slipped to S$1.7065, its lowest since early July. It started rallying after a spate of data showed the local economy was strengthening while global markets speculated the Federal Reserve may be nearing the end of its monetary tightening cycle.  \par \par \uc2 'The change in expectations was because of the FOMC statement which encouraged market players to pare back some extended positions in the U.S. dollar going into the new year,' said Thomas Lam, a treasury economist at United Overseas Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Lam said the major position-squaring came in the dollar/yen, which also lifted other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Singapore data released Friday showed non-oil domestic exports in November grew 14.0% from a year earlier to S$12.92 billion, beating a Dow Jones Newswires poll forecast of 11.4% growth.  \par \par \uc2 The data also boosted hopes that the trade-dependent economy will be able to sustain strong growth for a second quarter in a row after gross domestic product grew 7% in the third quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank said in a report that the stronger economy means the MAS will likely continue its current policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, which would trigger more gains for the Singapore dollar in the first half of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 On the Singapore government bond market, the 10-year bond yield was flat at 3.34%, while the two-year yield edged up one basis point to 2.77%, flattening the yield curve slightly in thin trade.  \par \par \uc2 The short end has been pressured by liquidity outflows at the end of the year, which has kept the overnight rate firm, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par