\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Rises To New 3-Mo High For 3rd Straight Day  \b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2005\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar climbed to a new three-month high for the third straight session in Asia late Thursday, mainly owing to speculator-led profit-taking on long U.S. dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond yields rose more in the short end than in the long end, leading to a flattening of the curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6676, down from S$1.6718 late Wednesday. It traded between S$1.6664 and S$1.6705 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 From early in the session, U.S. funds unwound long U.S. dollar positions against the Singapore dollar to take profit ahead of the year-end, thereby dragging the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down as low as S$1.6676 in the morning, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Another negative factor for the U.S. dollar against its Singaporean counterpart was Wednesday's U.S. data showing that the trade deficit widened by 4.4% to a fresh record high of US$68.89 billion in October from September's revised US$66 billion gap. The October figure was worse than economists' expectations for a US$63 billion deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakness versus the Singapore dollar appears to reflect a change in market sentiment away from the U.S. dollar after the U.S. Federal Reserve removed its reference to monetary policy being accommodative, which many players perceived as a signal that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is approaching the end of its interest-rate hike cycle.  \par \par \uc2 But, the U.S. dollar soon rebounded to near the psychologically important S$1.6700 level in the morning on rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to cap a further rise in the Singapore dollar versus the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Late in the session, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed its decline versus the Singapore dollar, dropping to as low as S$1.6664, its lowest level since Aug. 23, due to more selling of U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars by U.S. funds, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore government bond market, the yield curve flattened slightly in extremely thin markets, as local players were reluctant to build any positions ahead of the year-end.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Latest   Previous \par Overnight rate        2.8750%  3.0625% \par Two-year bond yield   2.75%    2.73% \par 10-year bond yield    3.35%    3.34% \par Two-year swap offer   3.19%    3.17% \par 10-year swap offer    3.58%    3.60% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  39 BPs   43 BPs \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4159; ron.harui@dowjones.com -Edited by Paul Baylis -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020051215e1cf005ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
