\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Down Late On Limited Upside; Bonds Weaker  \b0\par\par\uc2 471 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2005\par 08:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was slightly lower in thin trade Thursday as local players saw little incentive to push the local unit near a key resistance level.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the Singapore dollar has limited upside as the greenback is already close to its support level.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds were weaker with the long end leading losses, causing a steepening of the yield curve, they said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0725 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6861, up from S$1.6854 late Wednesday. It traded between S$1.6846 and S$1.6866.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar slipped to a seven-week low against the Singapore dollar earlier this week, but has consolidated after market players saw less room for the Singapore dollar to rally.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is pretty much rangebound ahead of the holidays," a dealer at a foreign bank said. Despite the yen's gains against the U.S. dollar and euro, players weren't interested to buy the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Sabrina Jacobs, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein, said the Singapore dollar has limited room to strengthen because the U.S. dollar is "very close to its support level of S$1.6300."  \par \par \uc2 She said fears the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the market pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar too high also kept players cautious.  \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank in a report said the Singapore dollar is likely to trade in line with the U.S. dollar's broad trend against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the rest of this year, and may actually weaken to S$1.6900 by the year-end.  \par \par \uc2 But early next year, it may strengthen to as much as S$1.6500 on renewed speculation that China may let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen ahead of the meeting of the G-7 industrialized nations in February.  \par \par \uc2 On the bond market, the 10-year bond slipped, tracking losses in U.S. Treasurys, while the two-year bond was little changed amid the recent easing of short-term rates. This led to a steepening of the yield curve for the second session in a row, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the yields of Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest   Previous \par Overnight rate        2.563%   2.375% \par Two-year bond yield   2.66%    2.65% \par 10-year bond yield    3.31%    3.29% \par Two-year swap offer   3.18%    3.15% \par 10-year swap offer    3.58%    3.54% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  40 BPs   39 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Genevieve Soledad [ 08-12-05 0738GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051208e1c8000h8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
