\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Singapore dollar, Philippine peso up  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2005\par 03:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 24 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar rose and the Korean won weakened on Thursday as the U.S. dollar steadied after recent falls, helped by markets having second thoughts about speculation that U.S. monetary tightening may soon end.  \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso <PHP=> hit a 6-\u189\'00\'BD   month peak of 54.17 per dollar, helped by foreign investment and remittances from Filipinos working overseas, although it pared some of those gains towards the close of trading.  \par \par \uc2  Traders had sold the U.S. dollar after minutes of the Federal Reserve's November policy meeting released on Tuesday suggested some board members were wary of raising rates too much.  \par \par \uc2  But by Thursday many in the market were veering to the view that they may have over-reacted and the Fed was merely signalling U.S. policy was no longer that accommodative.  \par \par \uc2  Some felt that even if the Fed paused early next year in a tightening cycle that began in June 2004, it could raise rates once again later in 2006.  \par \par \uc2  The yen <JPY=> came off Wednesday's highs around 118.20 per dollar, to trade around 118.65/70. That weighed on the South Korean won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> and the Thai baht <THB=>.  \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rally in the short term as markets unwind heavy speculative holdings of dollars.  \par \par \uc2  "For the time being, the won can strengthen because the dollar is going through a bit of an adjustment," Jan Lambregts, head of Asia-Pacific research at Rabobank, said.  \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. dollar will be underpinned by it's yield advantage in the longer term, he said.  \par \par \uc2  "On a six-month horizon, the yen will struggle to make gains. The dollar can still perform reasonably well here."  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE PUZZLE  \par \par \uc2  Traders continued to be puzzled by developments in Singapore's money markets, as the Singapore dollar <SGD=> rallied to a 4-week high of 1.6902 per U.S. dollar and money market rates eased from their highest levels in nearly 6 years.  \par \par \uc2  At Thursday's peak, the Singapore dollar was more than a cent up from the week's lows. Talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money markets could not be confirmed.  \par \par \uc2  James Malcolm, Deutsche Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, said the Singapore dollar's rally was on account of the yen's recovery.  \par \par \uc2  "It is just a product of the U.S. dollar losing steam and some lumpy flows," he said.  \par \par \uc2  Overnight money market rates <SGDF=> eased to about 2.5 percent from the day's high of 3 percent, as did other rates which had been rising for 3 weeks owing to several factors including the weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rising bank lending and speculation that the MAS wanted to keep rates tight.  \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, traders suspected the Singapore dollar's rally had led to rates being received in the money and fixed income markets, although no one was really sure.  \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   Change on the day at 0918 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.91         118.35       -0.47\par  Sing dlr        1.6918         1.6937       +0.11\par  Taiwan dlr      33.481         33.480       -0.00\par  Korean won     1037.70        1