\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Taiwan dollar, won higher but peso slips  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2005\par 04:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 14 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed on Monday as the Taiwan dollar and South Korean won hit multi-week highs as foreigners bought stocks, while the Philippine peso succumbed to profit taking after sharp gains in the past month.  \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=><KRW=KTFC> firmed about 0.4 percent to a 5-week peak against the dollar at 1,037.7, while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> strengthened about a third of a percent to a 4-week high at 33.372 per U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Sentiment towards the north Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was boosted by foreign buyers of the country's stocks. This is a contrast to last month, when heavy foreign selling of South Korean and Taiwan shares sent the won and Taiwan dollar to multi-month lows.  \par \par \uc2 Seoul's benchmark share index <.KS11> on Monday hit a record high and Taiwan's TAIEX stock index <.TWII> closed at a 5-week peak.  \par \par \uc2 "Korea and Taiwan are tracking tech stocks," said Philip Wee, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at DBS Bank in Singapore. "In the NDF market, the won and the Taiwan dollar are the most sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to equities."  \par \par \uc2 Foreign institutions bought a net 10.6 billion Taiwan dollars worth of local stocks on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 That brought their net purchases in November to a hefty 98.4 billion Taiwan dollars, more than offsetting the accumulated net selling in August, September and October.  \par \par \uc2 PESO SLIPS  \par \par \uc2 The peso <PHP=> rose as high as 54.38 per dollar, the strongest level since late May, before profit taking set in.  \par \par \uc2 It was last trading at about 54.65 per dollar, about 0.4 percent weaker than Friday's local close.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the early rally was seen as a good opportunity by local firms to sell pesos for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 The peso had been due a pull back after strong gains on hefty inflows of cash from Filipinos working overseas and upbeat sentiment about the economy, a Manila-based dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was oversold last week so we were due a technical bounce in the dollar," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Near-term, there could be a covering of short positions on the dollar but the bigger picture is for further peso strength in the next 1-2 weeks as remittances will continue to come in and we should get more positive news about the economy."  \par \par \uc2 Traders said there was talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar <SGD=>, although no actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had yet been seen.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hit a 4-month low at about 1.7045 per U.S. dollar on Friday, undermined by broad-based U.S. dollar strength and a break of key chart support levels.  \par \par \uc2 "There is talk that the MAS could check \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates at about 1.7025, so the market is wary about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a trader. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an intraday low of 1.7025 per U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were supported by speculation that China might allow the yuan to strengthen further <CNY=CFXS>.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. President George W. Bush visits Asia this week on a trip that includes Beijing, where he is expected to press for further \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is not expecting a near-term move in the yuan but is not discounting any surprises either," said Wee at DBS. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0812 GMT   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.02         117.80       -0.19\par  Sing dlr        1.7005         1.7014       +0.05\par  Taiwan dlr      33.387         33.473       +0.26\par  Korean won     1037.60        1041.40       +0.37\par  Baht             41.10          41.11       +0.02\par  Peso             54.62          54.42       -0.37\par  Rupiah        10000.00        9966.00       -0.34\par  Rupee            45.67          45.68       +0.01\par  Ringgit         3.7783         3.7782       -0.00\par  Yuan            8.0831         8.0856       +0.03\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       118.02         102.45      -13.19\par  Sing dlr        1.7005         1.6310       -4.09\par  Taiwan dlr      33.387         31.920       -4.39\par  Korean won     1037.60        1035.10       -0.24\par  Baht             41.10          38.88       -5.40\par  Peso             54.62          56.28       +3.04\par  Rupiah        10000.00        9290.00       -7.10\par  Rupee            45.67          43.47       -4.82\par  Ringgit         3.7783         3.8000       +0.57\par  Yuan            8.0831         8.2760       +2.39 ((Editing by\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Neil Fullick, Reuters Messaging: dhara.ranasinghe.reuters.com@reuters.net, +65 6870 3840))  \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight