\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Flat In Asia, Bonds Fall Led By Short End  \b0\par\par\uc2 506 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2005\par 09:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was little changed against the U.S. dollar in Asian trade Monday, shrugging off early losses after the U.S. unit weakened against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds eased, led by selling at the front end of the yield curve, which further led to a flattening of the 2-10 year curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0714 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.7010, nearly unchanged from S$1.7011 late Friday. It traded between S$1.7005 and S$1.7028 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 A dealer at a foreign bank said the Singapore dollar erased early losses after the U.S. dollar slipped against the euro and the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Despite fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may step in to stem the slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the past few days, said a second dealer.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar hit a four-month high of S$1.7045 during intraday trading Friday, but started easing in the late session that day.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as the Singapore dollar is trading within the band they don't seem to be worried," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar is currently estimated at 0.8% below the midpoint of the band, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, United Overseas Bank said it expects the local unit to focus on a raft of economic data due this week with October's non-oil domestic exports - or NODX - being key.  \par \par \uc2 The NODX data due Thursday are expected to show exports resuming a growth trend after faltering to a 0.4% on-year decline in September.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Dow Jones Newswires expect NODX to rise 4.2% on year in October because of improving electronics shipments and a recovery in pharmaceutical exports.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore government bond market, yields on the short end continued to lead the selloff in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on the government bonds have been rising because of selling in U.S. Treasurys while short positions in the Singapore dollar lately further squeezed liquidity from the front end, said a strategist at a foreign bank.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year bond yield rose to 2.68%, up by two basis points, and the 10-year bond yield edged up by one basis points to 3.18%.  \par \par \uc2 The following are yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Latest   Previous \par Overnight rate        2.50%    2.750% \par Two-year bond yield   2.68%    2.66% \par 10-year bond yield    3.18%    3.17% \par Two-year swap offer   3.10%    3.10% \par 10-year swap offer    3.55%    3.59% \par 2-10-yr swap spread:  45 BPs   49 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 14-11-05 0841GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051114e1be000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
