\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DEBT: Singapore Curve Flattening May Not Last   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Saeed Azhar   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2005\par 06:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is draining liquidity from the money market, pushing up short-term interest rates and flattening the yield curve to a level last seen in the middle of the year.   \par \par \uc2 The shape of the curve is odd at a time when the economy is strengthening from a period of slowdown. A strengthening economy generally acts to steepen the yield curve.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore will in coming weeks need to continue to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to meet its goal of gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar so the yield curve could flatten even further.   \par \par \uc2 But in the longer-term the stronger economy and a possible rise in yields on U.S. Treasurys, which Singapore's bond market closely tracks, will lead to a curve steepening.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say that volatility in the curve's shape makes it difficult for investors to take short-term positions on Singapore's curve movements alone.   \par \par \uc2 They say it makes more sense to execute trades that link the Singapore and U.S. yield curves. They say Singapore's strong economic performance means that the Singapore curve will lag any U.S. curve flattening and will outsteepen any U.S. curve steepening.   \par \par \uc2 Chia Woon Khien, a fixed-income strategist at UBS bank, has recommended investors do a box trade. That is, they should buy Singapore 2-10-year interest-rate swap steepener against a U.S. 2-10-year swap flattener.   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore 2-10-year spread is 68 basis points while the U.S. 2-10-year spread is 24 basis points, making the difference between the two 44 basis points. Chia said the target for this relative value trade is for a widening to 66 basis points over the next three months.   \par \par \uc2 She believes Singapore's term structure is unlikely to stay flat for long because its economy is strengthening.   \par \par \uc2 Singapore's industrial output rose more than expected in August due to robust growth in the pharmaceutical and transport-engineering sectors. This has prompted some economists to revise upward their economic growth forecasts for the third quarter.   \par \par \uc2 The data also likely mean that the economy is on track to meet the government's projected growth of 3.5%-4.5% for the whole year, moderating from last year's 8.4%, when the economy rebounded from a SARS outbreak in 2003.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     MAS Action Accelerated Flattening \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The recent flattening trend was partly driven by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap market, which led market participants to cover short positions in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , pushing up sharply the overnight rate.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The overnight rate hit a high of 2.35% on Sept. 23, its highest level since late 2001, and up from a year-low of 1.25% in January. It has averaged 1.75% so far this year. At the same time, yields on Singapore government bonds have remained relatively low, in line with long-term U.S. Treasury yields.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The spread between the two- and 10-year yields, a measure of the curve's shape, narrowed to 53 basis points Thursday from 82 basis points on August 30.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That is near to the 46-point level set in June which was the narrowest spread since the Asian financial crisis in 1997-98.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We see a flattening bias because there's a risk the overnight rate may rise to 2.5%," said Selena Ling, a fixed-income analyst at Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ling said that with strong resistance for the yield on 10-year bonds at 3.0%, the two-10-year spread could test the June low in coming weeks. The 10-year bond currently yields 2.86%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others say that the MAS will likely reduce its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market and allow liquidity in the short-term money market to rise after its semiannual policy review in the middle of October.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The MAS at that time will likely maintain its policy of targeting a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar, which could give the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a psychological boost and allow MAS to pull back from the market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore targets the exchange rate, rather than interest rates, as its monetary policy tool since its exports and imports amount to three times the city-state's S$184 billion economy.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Singapore dollar is currently 0.6% below the midpoint of its estimated trade-weighted band, United Overseas Bank said in a note Thursday. The U.S. dollar was last quoted at S$1.6893 at 0507 GMT.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Saeed Azhar is a money reporter in the Dow Jones Newswires Singapore bureau. Previously he covered Pakistan for Dow Jones)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Iain McDonald [ 30-09-05 