\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore curve inversion signalling tight policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2005\par 11:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 26 (Reuters) - Singapore's short-term interest rates have risen and the yield curve has inverted this month, signalling to markets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will retain its tightening bias at October's semi-annual policy review.  \par \par \uc2 Over the past two weeks, overnight rates in the low-yielding economy have climbed 55 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight rates <SGDDFIX=ABSG> were quoted on Monday at 2.57 percent, higher than a one-month rate of 2.38 and a six-month rate of 2.34 percent -- a negatively sloping money market curve.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said this inversion of the curve suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to push up short-term rates, to signal that its tightening bias was intact.  \par \par \uc2 Monetary policy in Singapore is run through the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The MAS conducts policy by guiding the Singapore dollar <SGD=> within a secret trade-weighed band and, since April last year, has tightened policy through a gradual appreciation of the band.  \par \par \uc2 "Heading into the beginning of October, the MAS will start guiding the Sing dollar towards where it wants it to be," said Chia Woon Khien, an interest rates strategist with UBS in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher ahead of its mid-October policy review was forcing speculators and traders with short Sing dollar positions to borrow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing up rates out to a month, Chia said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been trading in the strong half of what economists estimated to be its policy band earlier this year when expectations were high that China would revalue the yuan, bringing speculative cash into Asia.  \par \par \uc2 The Sing dollar weakened to the bottom of the band after the size of China's July 21 yuan revaluation disappointed markets.  \par \par \uc2 Economists said it was unlikely the MAS will accelerate the pace of tightening, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be allowed to move up to the top end of the band. The MAS has not disclosed the rate at which it has allowed the Sing dollar's policy band to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 The Sing dollar was quoted around 1.6890 per dollar on Monday. Some analysts estimated it was still trading about 0.75 percent weaker than the mid-point of the trade-weighted band, so it had room to rise going into the review.  \par \par \uc2 "With the Singapore dollar trading at the weaker end of its trading band, we expect front-end Singapore rates to keep pace with the gradual increase in the U.S., unless in the unlikely event MAS drops its appreciation stance," Adrian Khoo, BNP Paribas's interest rates strategist said in a note.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts said markets were forcing short-term rates up as investors sought a better return to justify buying the Singapore dollar in exchange for the higher-yielding U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 One interest rate trader said the MAS had been spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps to push up \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \uc2 "Previously, we saw them leave more liquidity in the market because the economic numbers were printing quite poorly," he said. "But, over the last few weeks they want to keep rates a bit firmer. They don't really need to have this liquidity bias any more and a loose policy can push inflation up."  \par \par \uc2 The economy reported a quarterly contraction in output between January and March but has since rebounded, with the manufacturing sector expanding at a healthy rate and inflation showing signs of picking up in August.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-SINGAPORE-RATES|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|SI|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050926e19q000on\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
