\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Singapore To Keep Policy Tight  \b0\par\par\uc2 985 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2005\par 15:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 By Saeed Azhar  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires column \par   SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to reaffirm a tight monetary policy at October's semiannual review as persistently high oil prices mean it will be wary of inflation pressures, analysts say. \par   Maintaining a tight policy, which has been in place since April 2004 and targets modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar, will also reflect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  confidence that the economy has regained a stable footing after an annualized contraction in the first quarter. \par   'If they view the risk to growth as very limited, and price pressures are translating into higher inflation, then the intended stance of monetary policy would remain that of modest and gradual appreciation,' said Sanjay Mathur, an economist at UBS Bank. \par   MAS has only said the monetary policy review will be in October, and analysts expect it to be in the second week of the month. \par   For much of this year, prices in Singapore have been very subdued, picking up only in the last couple of months as record high oil prices start being passed through to consumers. For instance, Singapore's transport operators recently increased bus and taxi fares to protect their profit margins. \par   The consumer price index fell in June before picking up to a 0.1% year-on-year rise in the seven months ended July. And in August, inflation rose faster than expected at a 0.7% year-on-year pace although the CPI registered a still meek 0.2% for the January-August period. In the first half, the inflationary impact from higher oil prices was partly offset by lower car prices and competition among gasoline retailers. \par   MAS has forecast inflation of between 0% and 1.0% for 2005. \par    Policy Stance Likely Unch In Next 12-18 Mos - UOB \par   Jimmy Koh, an economist at United Overseas Bank, thinks the MAS will maintain a tight policy as inflation is 'creeping up.' \par   In Singapore's case, this means allowing the local dollar to strengthen. The republic won't be alone in adopting such a stance, he added, noting that other Asian economies are also allowing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to strengthen to overcome the impact of expensive oil imports. \par   Koh doesn't expect the current policy stance to change over the next 12 to 18 months as Singapore isn't likely to be hit by recession although economic growth may slow. \par   Structural changes in the economy, such as a move away from electronics to biomedical manufacturing, and toward a greater reliance on financial sector growth will help cushion Singapore from a major slowdown, he said. \par   Singapore uses the exchange rate, rather than interest rates, as its monetary policy tool because trade amounts to three times the city-state's S$184 billion economy - one of the highest such ratios in the world. \par   The MAS operates a managed float regime for Singapore, which allows the trade-weighted exchange rate to fluctuate within an undisclosed policy band, and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent it from breaching the band. \par   The last monetary policy review was in April, a few months before China and Malaysia revalued their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and loosened their link to the U.S. dollar. Since then, however, both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have strengthened very marginally to the disappointment of many \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers expecting a sharp rise in regional units. \par   Daniel Hui, an economist at JPMorgan, said that historically, the MAS has only turned accommodative in the wake of major external shocks that posed a significant risk to Singapore's growth. \par    Last Shift To Neutral In 2003 \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The last shift to a neutral policy came in 2003 when the city state and its regional trading partners were reeling from the outbreak of SARS virus. 'While Singapore began the year on the weak side and growth indicators firmed only at the end of the second quarter, the recent external demand recovery should ensure a solid second half' for the Singapore economy, said Hui. Gross domestic product grew 5.2% in the second quarter, recovering from a lower-than-expected 2.7% growth in the first, when output from the volatile biomedical sector slumped. Thus, although the economy won't grow as fast as 2004's 8.4% when it rebounded from the previous year's SARS outbreak, GDP could still expand as much as 4.5% this year, according to the government. As the policy review draws near, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to push the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar back near the midpoint of the band which may tighten liquidity as players short of Singapore dollars cover their positions. At 0821 GMT Friday, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6837, significantly higher from S$1.6449 around the April policy review. Against its trade-weighted exchange rate, the Singapore dollar is likely about 0.6% below the estimated midpoint of the band, said United Overseas Bank, which reckons the lower end of the band is at S$1.6988 and upper end at S$1.6322. The midpoint is likely at S$1.6655, assuming 2% on each side, UOB said. Strength recently seen in economic indicators hasn't been translated into a higher \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because of a resurgent U.S. dollar and weakness in the Indonesian rupiah, which has generally been a drag on most other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Still, forecasters are generally positive about the Singapore dollar's medium term prospects. Citigroup forecasts the U.S. dollar at S$1.6500 by the end of the year, JPMorgan is looking S$1.6200 and the United Overseas Bank projects the U.S. dollar to weaken to S$1.6400. -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com (Saeed Azhar is a money reporter in the Dow Jones Newswires Singapore bureau. Previously he covered Pakistan for Dow Jones) -Edited by Stephen Wright & Lim Mui Khi -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050923e19n00913\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
