\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Down Late After Fed Signals More Tightening  \b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2005\par 09:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was weaker against its U.S. counterpart in late Asian trade Wednesday, after the U.S. Federal Reserve signaled it would maintain its measured pace of monetary tightening.  \par \par \uc2 Short-dated government bonds slipped in response to the Fed's decision to raise rates, while the rest of the curve was relatively steady, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6809, up from S$1.6775 Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6805 and S$1.6841.  \par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday, the Federal Open Market Committee voted to hike the key federal fund's rate by 25 basis points to 3.75% and indicated in its accompanying statement that it would continue to tighten policy at a measured pace.  \par \par \uc2 The hawkish statement from the FOMC spurred some U.S. dollar short-covering against the Singapore dollar, lifting the U.S. unit to S$1.6841, its highest since Aug. 31, very early in the session.  \par \par \uc2 But Singapore companies sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around S$1.6841, apparently to hedge their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposure, dragging the U.S. unit back down as low as S$1.6806 in the afternoon, dealers said. Talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the U.S. dollar's rise beyond S$1.6841 was another factor that pulled the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, said traders.  \par \par \uc2 But technical analysis show the overall tone of the U.S. dollar is still positive, said United Overseas Bank in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 "Key resistance remains at S$1.6860 and a move above this level will lead likely to higher acceleration," UOB said, adding "it is unlikely that we will move below S$1.6765 from here."  \par \par \uc2 As a short-term trading strategy, UOB recommended buying the U.S. dollar at S$1.6800, and placing stop-loss orders below S$1.6765  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Singapore government bonds rose moderately in the short end but edged down in the long end, resulting in a flatter yield curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "It looks like the flattening trend is back," in the wake of the FOMC meeting, said a Singapore dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Fed rate hike put upward pressure on short-term yields, but lingering uncertainty over future rate direction prevented longer-dated yields from rising too much.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       2.2500%    2.1300% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.24%      2.19% \par    10-year bond yield   2.92%      2.93% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.41%      2.35% \par    10-year swap offer   3.20%      3.16% \par    2-10-yr swap spread  79 BPs     81 BPs \par  \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par   \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Gauri Bhatia [ 21-09-05 0722GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050921e19l000b0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
