\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Edges Up Late On US Dlr Profit-Taking  \b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2005\par 09:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar edged higher in late Asian trade Wednesday on U.S. dollar profit-taking sparked by rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The yield curve of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds flattened slightly, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6790, down from S$1.6800 Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6767 and S$1.6818 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 In the wake of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Tuesday to curb the U.S. dollar's rise against its Singaporean counterpart, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened Wednesday's session with a negative tone versus the local dollar, dealers said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 -based players used the apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an excuse to take profits on their long U.S. dollar positions against the Singapore dollar, thereby pushing the U.S. unit down as low as S$1.6767 in the morning, dealers added.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the MAS' rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a typical pattern that occurs when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar comes close to the weaker boundary of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nominal effective exchange rate, or NEER, band.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS doesn't disclose its policy band, but Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi economist Wong Keng Siong in a research report Wednesday said he estimates that the Singapore dollar weakened late Tuesday to around 0.85% below the policy midpoint.  \par \par \uc2 "By logical extension, the likelihood is that the Singapore dollar would firm toward the S$1.6700 per (U.S.) dollar mark later today," Wong said.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the U.S. dollar profit-taking early in the session, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon rebounded to near the psychologically important S$1.6800 level in the afternoon, supported by improved sentiment spurred by Tuesday's release of a narrower-than-expected U.S. trade deficit for July.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. deficit in international trade of goods and services contracted to US$57.94 billion in July from June's revised US$59.49 billion. The July figure was smaller than the US$60 billion trade gap which Wall Street had expected.  \par \par \uc2 In Singapore's government bond market, the yield curve flattened by around five basis points, as the two-year yield rose on tight liquidity while the 10-year yield tracked the 10-year U.S. Treasurys yield lower, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting this tight liquidity, the overnight interbank rate remained high at 2.0625% in Asia late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 OCBC Bank, in a weekly report, said the tight liquidity conditions are suspected to be linked to the MAS' policy need to curb speculative interest to sell the Singapore dollar against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "We suspect that current firmness in Sibor (Singapore interbank offered rate) levels may persist until the October monetary policy review when the future path of the SGD monetary policy becomes clearer," OCBC said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       2.0625%    2.0625% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.17%      2.14% \par    10-year bond yield   2.89%      2.91% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.34%      2.35% \par    10-year swap offer   3.14%      3.1