\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Down Late As Worries Over Katrina Diminish  \b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2005\par 09:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar slipped in late Asian trade Tuesday on U.S. dollar purchases spurred by diminishing worries over the negative impact from Hurricane Katrina on the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds also fell along the curve, mainly on Monday's weakness in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0635 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at its intraday high of S$1.6800, up from S$1.6747 Monday. Its intraday low was S$1.6766.  \par \par \uc2 Bearish sentiment toward U.S. economic growth prospects that had accumulated in the wake of Hurricane Katrina continued to decline, prompting Singapore players to buy U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars early in Tuesday's Asian session.  \par \par \uc2 Some Singaporean companies also bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helping to lift the U.S. dollar as high as S$1.6800 in the afternoon, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 While the hurricane caused extensive damage to the U.S. Gulf Coast, the storm is unlikely to prevent the Federal Reserve from hiking interest rates, even at the Federal Open Market Committee's next meeting on Sept. 20.  \par \par \uc2 But lingering speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore may not tolerate excessive weakness of the Singapore dollar capped the U.S. dollar's upside in the Asian session, dealers said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is rumored to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Friday and Monday to curb the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise beyond the psychologically important S$1.6800 resistance, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 From a technical analysis perspective, however, the U.S. dollar is likely to extend its gains versus the Singapore dollar in Tuesday's global session, said United Overseas Bank in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 "Having said that, previous attempts to move higher have failed at S$1.6800 and even a (U.S. dollar) move above this level will likely be limited to S$1.6820-S$1.6825," UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore government bond market, yields rose between four and eight basis points along the curve, in line with higher yields on U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. The 10-year Treasury yield hit a post-Katrina high overnight as many investors' worst fears about the overall economic impact of the hurricane subsided.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, new corporate bond issuance in the form of S$500 million worth of bonds from Hongkong Land Holdings, which plans to sell the bonds this week, put downward pressure on local bond prices.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       2.0625%    2.0000% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.17%      2.09% \par    10-year bond yield   2.91%      2.87% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.35%      2.28% \par    10-year swap offer   3.19%      3.08% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 84 BPs     80 BPs \par  \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par  \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Gauri Bhatia [ 13-09-05 0713GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050913e19d000a6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal33