\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Relatively Steady Vs US Dollar Late  \b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2005\par 10:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was relatively steady late in Asia Wednesday, caught between sales of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for non-U.S. dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prospects of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's fall.  \par \par \uc2 Short-dated government bonds slipped on tighter liquidity, causing the curve to flatten, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0655 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6765, almost unchanged from S$1.6766 late Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6744 and S$1.6780 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 From early in the session, the U.S. dollar steadily advanced against the local dollar, reaching an intraday high of S$1.6780 in the afternoon, as some speculators sold the Singapore dollar for non-U.S. dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 'It's all cross (buying) plays against the Singapore dollar,' said a Singapore dealer.  \par \par \uc2 But lingering speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the U.S. dollar's rise against the Singapore dollar weighed down the U.S. unit later in the session.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumored to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday evening to curb the U.S. dollar's rise versus the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 'Signs of MAS action appear to be capping near-term upside. We expect the immediate USD/SGD upside to be limited from here,' JP Morgan Chase Bank said in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 In its weekly report, OCBC Bank said it expects the U.S. dollar to hold on to support at S$1.6700, which is roughly 50% retracement of the rise from the May low of S$1.6339.  \par \par \uc2 But OCBC added that any U.S. dollar fall below the S$1.6700 support level would target next support in the S$1.6650-S$1.6660 region in the coming days.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds weakened at the short end of the curve, mainly due to a rise in the overnight interbank rate to 2.1250% Wednesday from 2.0625% Tuesday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 With yields on long-term securities rising less than those on short-dated bonds, however, the yield curve flattened slightly. The spread between two- and 10-year local bond yields narrowed to 77 basis points Wednesday from 79 basis points Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       2.1250%    2.0625% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.06%      2.03% \par    10-year bond yield   2.83%      2.82% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.28%      2.26% \par    10-year swap offer   3.08%      3.05% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 80 BPs     79 BPs \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com -Edited by Lena Lee -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050907e197004bm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
