\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Down Late; Continued Strength In Oil Prices  \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2005\par 09:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar slipped against its U.S. counterpart in late Asian trading Wednesday, undermined by the unrelenting march ofoil prices during the session.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices were flat to higher across the curve, more or less in line with Tuesday's rise in U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 At 0637 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6865, up from S$1.6833 late Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6829 and S$1.6871 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   The U.S. dollar, which rose to a fresh five-week high of S$1.6880 in New York late Tuesday, fell as low as S$1.6829 on expectations    of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reaffirmed Wednesday that its policy of seeking a gradual and modest appreciation of the Singapore dollar remains unchanged. \par   'MAS monetary policy as articulated in its Monetary Policy Statement of April 12, (2005), remains. The next policy review will be in October,' the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said in an e-mail to Dow Jones Newswires. \par   Its response came after economists raised concerns that Singapore's economy and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be feeling the pressure of the Indonesian crisis as well as record-high oil prices. \par   Still, the U.S. dollar steadily rebounded against the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the rest of the session, reaching as high as S$1.6871 late in the afternoon, as oil prices continued their rally amid  fears over possible damages to oil refineries along the U.S. Gulf Coast from Hurricane Katrina. \par   Growing worries that high oil prices could adversely affect economic growth in Asian countries have contributed to a recent weakness in regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including the Singapore dollar. \par   The Nymex October crude futures contract surged as high as US$70.63 per barrel on Access trading in Asia Wednesday. At 0637 GMT, it was at US$70.39 per barrel, still up from Tuesday's close of US$69.81 a barrel. \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       1.9375%    1.9375% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.05%      2.05% \par    10-year bond yield   2.82%      2.87% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.25%      2.28% \par    10-year swap offer   3.06%      3.10% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 81 BPs     82 BPs \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com -Edited by Ven Ram -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050831e18v005sf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
