\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late On Yen;Off Early Highs On MAS Fears  \b0\par\par\uc2 479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2005\par 09:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose slightly against its U.S counterpart late Friday following gains in the Japanese yen, but lost some of its early shine on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds rose at the long end on overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys and were slightly weaker at the front end, which flattened the yield curve.  \par \par \uc2 At 0637 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6474, down from S$1.6480 late Thursday. The U.S. unit slipped to S$1.6440 Thursday, its lowest level since May 11, but suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the greenback in late trade.  \par \par \uc2 Friday, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid to S$1.6462 early in the session, when the Japanese yen was rallying ahead of the data release for gross domestic product in the second quarter. The U.S. dollar had dipped to Y109.59 against the dollar in the early session, before bouncing back to Y109.94.  \par \par \uc2 A trader said the Singapore dollar could have gained more, but fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  capped the rise.  \par \par \uc2 The local unit has been bolstered by stronger-than-expected 5.2% growth in the economy in the second quarter from a year ago.  \par \par \uc2 United Overseas Bank said in its technical analysis that the U.S. dollar's medium-term trend is bearish, and it may hit a low of S$1.6400. A move back above S$1.6530 will suggest that a recovery for the U.S. dollar is underway, the bank said.  \par \par \uc2 The next major economic data release for Singapore is on Wednesday, when the Ministry of Trade and Industry announces non-oil domestic exports for July. If the data are stronger than market expectations, they may trigger more gains for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Daniel Hui, economist at JPMorgan, said Singapore's exports should show a modest bounce in July, but a strong recovery is likely to come only in August on a possible rebound in global electronics demand.  \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan expects month-on-month seasonally adjusted non-oil exports growth of 2.5%. It expects on-year growth of 1.2% - a conservative projection compared with broader market estimates of 3.9%.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       1.7500%    1.7500% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.07%      2.06% \par    10-year bond yield   2.79%      2.83% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.26%      2.26% \par    10-year swap offer   3.04%      3.03% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 78 BPs     78 BPs \par  \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par  \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lena Lee [ 12-08-05 0729GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050812e18c000am\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlp